---
/**
 * Overview: a short summary that is always visible, then the full write-up in a native
 * <details> element ("Read more"). The full text is in the page's HTML (good for search
 * engines and readers without JavaScript) and the toggle needs no script.
 *
 * Every figure here comes from the original project's data or DATA_NOTES.md, or is
 * computed from that data at build time (src/lib/gapmap.ts).
 */
import { KEY_FIGURES } from '../../lib/gapmap';

const k = KEY_FIGURES;
const fmt1 = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString('en-US', { minimumFractionDigits: 1, maximumFractionDigits: 1 });
const fmt0 = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString('en-US', { maximumFractionDigits: 0 });
---

<section class="gm-overview" aria-labelledby="gm-overview-heading">
  <h2 id="gm-overview-heading" class="visually-hidden">Overview</h2>
  <p class="gm-overview__summary">
    The Washington Library Gap Map shows school librarian staffing in each of Washington’s {k.count} school districts,
    using staffing data that districts report to the Office of Superintendent of Public Instruction (OSPI) for the
    2015–16 through 2024–25 school years. In 2024–25, {k.zeroLatest} of the {k.count} districts ({k.zeroPctLatest}%) reported
    no librarian on staff.
  </p>

  <details class="gm-overview__more">
    <summary>Read more</summary>
    <div class="gm-overview__body">
      <h3>What the tool shows</h3>
      <p>
        The map shades each district by its librarian staffing in 2024–25: at least one full-time-equivalent librarian, a
        part-time librarian, or none. Selecting a district, on the map, through the search box, or from the table, opens
        a report with its staffing figures and its year-by-year history next to the averages for its locale group and for
        the state.
      </p>
      <p>
        The locale charts compare Washington’s six locale groups, from Distant Rural to Large Urban, in any year from
        2015–16 to 2024–25, and the table lists every district and can be sorted by any column.
      </p>

      <h3>Why it was made</h3>
      <p>
        OSPI publishes district staffing data each year. This tool brings ten years of that publicly available data
        together in one place, so anyone can look up a single district, compare it with others, and see how staffing has
        changed over time.
      </p>

      <h3>Changes from 2015–16 to 2024–25</h3>
      <p>
        Statewide, librarian staffing fell from {fmt1(k.fteFirst)} full-time-equivalent positions in 2015–16 to
        {' '}{fmt1(k.fteLatest)} in 2024–25, a decline of about {fmt0(Math.abs(k.fteChangePct))}%. Over the same period, the number of
        districts reporting no librarian rose from {k.zeroFirst} ({k.zeroPctFirst}%) to {k.zeroLatest} ({k.zeroPctLatest}%). Among the
        {' '}{k.districtsWithLibrarian} districts that reported a librarian in 2024–25, the median was about
        {' '}{fmt0(k.medianStudentsPerLibrarian)} students per librarian.
      </p>

      <h3>Differences by locale</h3>
      <p>
        Districts are grouped using the National Center for Education Statistics (NCES) locale codes. In 2024–25, 88% of
        Distant Rural districts (123 of 139) reported no librarian, compared with 31% of Suburban districts (15 of 49).
        Distant Rural districts make up nearly half of Washington’s districts, so statewide averages are weighted toward
        them.
      </p>

      <h3>Context</h3>
      <p>
        The original project also looked at reading results over the same years. On Washington’s Smarter Balanced
        English language arts test, the statewide share of fourth graders meeting the reading standard was 56.7% in
        2015–16 and 49.4% in 2024–25, according to OSPI; no test was given in 2019–20 because of COVID-19. On the National
        Assessment of Educational Progress (NAEP), Washington’s lead over the national average in reading narrowed after
        2017 in both grades, and in fourth grade Washington scored slightly below the national average in 2019 and 2022.
      </p>
      <p>
        Reading results and librarian staffing both changed over this period, but this data cannot show cause and effect:
        it does not show whether changes in staffing affected reading results. Reading results depend on many factors,
        including family income, which this analysis does not account for.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>

<style>
  .gm-overview {
    max-width: var(--prose-width);
  }
  .gm-overview__summary {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }
  .gm-overview__more summary {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    cursor: pointer;
    list-style: none;
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    color: var(--color-link);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-color: var(--color-link-underline);
    text-decoration-thickness: var(--underline-rest);
    text-underline-offset: 0.25em;
  }
  .gm-overview__more summary::-webkit-details-marker {
    display: none;
  }
  .gm-overview__more summary::before {
    content: '+';
    font-weight: var(--weight-normal);
    width: 0.8em;
  }
  .gm-overview__more[open] summary::before {
    content: '−';
  }
  .gm-overview__more summary:hover {
    text-decoration-color: var(--color-link-hover-mark);
    text-decoration-thickness: var(--underline-hover);
  }
  .gm-overview__body {
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    padding-top: var(--space-2);
    border-top: var(--hairline) solid var(--color-hairline);
  }
  .gm-overview__body h3 {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    margin: var(--space-6) 0 var(--space-2);
  }
  .gm-overview__body p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
</style>
